{"cuisine/guilin-cuisine":{"t":"cuisine","r":"cuisine/guilin-cuisine","n":"Guilin cuisine","c":"#f0b340","crumb":[["Asia","cuisines#asia"],["Chinese cuisine","cuisine/chinese-cuisine"]],"sub":"Regional cuisine in China","intro":[["Guilin, formerly romanized as Kweilin, is a prefecture-level city in the northeast of China's Guangxi Zhuang Autonomous Region. It is situated on the west bank of the Li River and borders Hunan to the north. The city has long been renowned for its scenery of karst topography."],["Guilin is one of China's most popular tourist destinations. A famous Chinese saying about the landscape of Guilin is \"Guilin's scenery is the best in the world.\" The State Council of China has designated Guilin a National Famous Historical and Cultural City, doing so in the first edition of the list."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"guilin-cuisine"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Guilin_cuisine"}],"sections":[{"h":"Recipe sites","empty":"None yet","tryLinks":[["Chinese cuisine","cuisine/chinese-cuisine"]]},{"h":"Part of","links":[["Chinese cuisine","cuisine/chinese-cuisine"]]}],"cont":"Asian cuisine","img":{"src":"/labs/cuisines/img/thumb/f/fb/View_of_Guilin_from_Elephant_Trunk_Hill.jpg/500px-View_of_Guilin_from_Elephant_Trunk_Hill.jpg","w":500,"h":281,"by":"Shujianyang","lic":"CC0","licUrl":"http://creativecommons.org/publicdomain/zero/1.0/deed.en","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:View_of_Guilin_from_Elephant_Trunk_Hill.jpg"}},"cuisine/livonian-cuisine":{"t":"cuisine","r":"cuisine/livonian-cuisine","n":"Livonian cuisine","c":"#cf5f87","crumb":[["Europe","cuisines#europe"],["Cuisine"]],"sub":"Regional cuisine in Latvia","intro":[["Livonian cuisine (in Livonian livõd kek) consists of the cuisine of Livonia and the Livonians, and is characterized by the rich use of locally available foods: Livonians are a coastal people – since they acquire the bulk of their food from the sea, Livonian families have historically eaten a great deal of fish, including European flounder (Livonian: liestād), herring (ēriņ), salted herring, and cod (tūrska)."],["In recent years, various projects have been launched to publicise Livonian cuisine (and culture)."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"livonian-cuisine"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Livonian_cuisine"}],"sections":[{"h":"Recipe sites","empty":"None yet","tryLinks":[]}],"cont":"European cuisine","img":{"src":"/labs/cuisines/img/thumb/a/a5/Suitsuskumbria_ja_suitsulest.jpg/500px-Suitsuskumbria_ja_suitsulest.jpg","w":500,"h":331,"by":"Raul Kern","lic":"CC BY-SA 4.0","licUrl":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Suitsuskumbria_ja_suitsulest.jpg"}},"cuisine/tuscan-cuisine":{"t":"cuisine","r":"cuisine/tuscan-cuisine","n":"Tuscan cuisine","c":"#cf5f87","crumb":[["Europe","cuisines#europe"],["Italian cuisine","cuisine/italian-cuisine"]],"sub":"Regional cuisine in Italy","intro":[["Tuscan cuisine comprises the culinary traditions of the Tuscan region in Italy, celebrated for its simplicity and focus on fresh, high-quality ingredients like olive oil, legumes, and meats. Rooted in the cucina povera (Italian for 'cuisine of the poor'), it emphasizes seasonal ingredients and straightforward flavors over complex sauces and spices."],["Tuscany is also home to some of the most famous wines in the world such as Chianti, Brunello di Montalcino, and Vino Nobile di Montepulciano."],["Bread plays a very important role in Tuscan cuisine. One specialty of Tuscan cuisine is a white, plain, unsalted bread. This bread accompanies all foods. This bread has its origin in the 16th century when salt was heavily taxed."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"tuscan-cuisine"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tuscan_cuisine"}],"sections":[{"h":"Dishes","count":1,"links":[["Ribollita","dish/ribollita"]]},{"h":"Recipe sites","count":2,"rows":[{"name":"Juls' Kitchen","color":"#55b8ad","shape":"site","sub":"en.julskitchen.com","note":"Tuscan cook and author Giulia Scarpaleggia's blog of Tuscan home recipes.","url":"https://en.julskitchen.com","suggested":true,"kind":"blog"},{"name":"Divina Cucina","color":"#55b8ad","shape":"site","sub":"divinacucina.com","note":"Florence-based cooking teacher Judy Witts Francini's site on Tuscan food.","url":"https://divinacucina.com/?p=3266","suggested":true,"kind":"blog"}]},{"h":"Cookbooks","count":1,"rows":[{"name":"The Tuscan Sun Cookbook","color":"#f0b340","shape":"book","sub":"Frances Mayes, Edward Mayes","note":"Recipes from the authors' kitchen and neighbors in Cortona.","url":"https://openlibrary.org/isbn/9780307885289","suggested":true,"isbn":"9780307885289"}]},{"h":"Key ingredients","links":[["Brassica oleracea var. palmifolia","ingredient/brassica-oleracea-var-palmifolia"],["Kidney bean","ingredient/kidney-bean"],["Phaseolus vulgaris","ingredient/phaseolus-vulgaris"]],"note":"Most used across its dishes."},{"h":"Part of","links":[["Italian cuisine","cuisine/italian-cuisine"]]}],"cont":"European cuisine","img":{"src":"/labs/cuisines/img/thumb/8/8b/Ribollita_toscana.JPG/500px-Ribollita_toscana.JPG","w":500,"h":375,"by":"Ithunn","lic":"CC BY-SA 3.0","licUrl":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/3.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ribollita_toscana.JPG","of":"Ribollita"}},"cuisine/mvskoke-foodways":{"t":"cuisine","r":"cuisine/mvskoke-foodways","n":"Mvskoke foodways","c":"#94bd5c","crumb":[["The Americas","cuisines#the-americas"],["Native American cuisine","cuisine/native-american-cuisine"]],"sub":"Indigenous foodways, Eastern Oklahoma","names":"Mvskoke, Muscogee (Creek)","intro":[["Corn runs through Mvskoke cooking. ",["Osafke","dish/sofkee"],", or sofkee, is a drink made from hominy corn and lye, the lye dripped through hardwood ash, ideally from black jack or oak. Cvtvhakv are blue dumplings of corn dough colored with the ash of burned purple pea hulls, and grape dumplings use wild possum grapes picked in fall."],["Indian pumpkin, a squash Mvskoke ancestors have grown for thousands of years, is fried with sugar until it caramelizes. The Mvskoke Food Sovereignty Initiative grows old osafke corn and Indian pumpkin from saved seed, and elders teach young people to make osafke."]],"src":"note","sources":[{"name":"ICT, Traditional foods for modern Indians","url":"https://ictnews.org/archive/traditional-foods-for-modern-indians/"},{"name":"SARE, Traditional Mvskoke Foods Recovery Project","url":"https://projects.sare.org/project-reports/cs11-084/"}],"actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"mvskoke-foodways"}],"sections":[{"h":"Dishes","count":1,"links":[["Sofkee","dish/sofkee"]]},{"h":"Recipe sites","empty":"None yet","tryLinks":[["Native American cuisine","cuisine/native-american-cuisine"]]},{"h":"Key ingredients","links":[["Hominy","ingredient/hominy"]],"note":"Most used across its dishes."},{"h":"Part of","links":[["Native American cuisine","cuisine/native-american-cuisine"]]},{"h":"Mentioned in","links":[["Hominy","ingredient/hominy"]]}],"cont":"Cuisine of the Americas"},"ingredient/mirepoix-cuisine":{"t":"ingredient","r":"ingredient/mirepoix-cuisine","n":"Mirepoix (cuisine)","c":"#c9b8dd","crumb":[["Ingredients","ingredients"]],"sub":"Ingredient in 1 dish across 2 cuisines","intro":[["A mirepoix is a mixture of diced vegetables cooked with fat (usually butter) for a long time on low heat without colouring or browning. The ingredients are not sautéed or otherwise hard-cooked, because the intention is to sweeten rather than caramelise them. Historically including various meats before settling at its current meaning as a vegetable base, mirepoix is a long-standing part of French cuisine and is the flavour base for a wide variety of dishes, including stocks, soups, stews, and sauces."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"ingredient-mirepoix-cuisine"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mirepoix_(cuisine)"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's cooked with","count":2,"groups":[["French cuisine","cuisine/french-cuisine",[["Blanquette de veau","dish/blanquette-de-veau"]]],["New Caledonian cuisine","cuisine/new-caledonian-cuisine",[["Blanquette de veau","dish/blanquette-de-veau"]]]]},{"h":"Often cooked with","links":[["Mirepoix","ingredient/mirepoix"],["Veal","ingredient/veal"]]}],"img":{"src":"/labs/cuisines/img/thumb/4/44/Mirepoix_on_cutting_board.jpg/500px-Mirepoix_on_cutting_board.jpg","w":500,"h":341,"by":"Pigup","lic":"CC BY-SA 3.0","licUrl":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/3.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mirepoix_on_cutting_board.jpg"}},"dish/cassatella-di-sant-agata":{"t":"dish","r":"dish/cassatella-di-sant-agata","n":"Cassatella di sant'Agata","c":"#ede6da","crumb":[["Dishes","dishes"]],"sub":"Dish from Italian cuisine","dot":"#cf5f87","intro":[["Cassatella di sant'Agata is a traditional Sicilian pastry from Catania made during the Festival of Saint Agatha, held from February 3rd to February 5th each year."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"dish-cassatella-di-sant-agata"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cassatella_di_sant'Agata"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's from","links":[["Italian cuisine","cuisine/italian-cuisine"]]},{"h":"Ingredients","count":10,"links":[["Candied peel","ingredient/candied-peel"],["Ceramic glaze","ingredient/ceramic-glaze"],["Fruit juice","ingredient/fruit-juice"],["Icing","ingredient/icing"],["Liqueur","ingredient/liqueur"],["Marzipan","ingredient/marzipan"],["Ricotta","ingredient/ricotta"],["Sponge cake","ingredient/sponge-cake"],["Succade","ingredient/succade"],["Sugar","ingredient/sugar"]]},{"h":"Cook it","rows":[{"name":"Pasta Grannies","color":"#55b8ad","shape":"site","sub":"pastagrannies.substack.com","note":"Vicky Bennison's long-running video series filming Italian grandmothers making regional pasta by hand.","url":"https://pastagrannies.substack.com/p/pasta-grannies-is-now-on-substack","suggested":true,"kind":"youtube"},{"name":"Recipes from Italy","color":"#55b8ad","shape":"site","sub":"recipesfromitaly.com","note":"Traditional Italian recipes written by Italian home cooks, organized by region and course.","url":"https://recipesfromitaly.com","suggested":true,"kind":"blog"},{"name":"Manu's Menu","color":"#55b8ad","shape":"site","sub":"manusmenu.com","note":"Italian-born home cook Manuela sharing classic family and regional Italian dishes.","url":"https://www.manusmenu.com/about/about-this-site","suggested":true,"kind":"blog"}]},{"h":"More from Italian cuisine","links":[["Acquacotta","dish/acquacotta"],["Agliata","dish/agliata"],["Agnolotti","dish/agnolotti"],["Antipasto","dish/antipasto"],["Arancini","dish/arancini"],["Biscotti","dish/biscotti"],["Bolinhos de bacalhau","dish/bolinhos-de-bacalhau"],["Bombolone","dish/bombolone"],["Budino","dish/budino"],["Cabbage roll","dish/cabbage-roll"],["Caesar salad","dish/caesar-salad"],["Cannelloni","dish/cannelloni"],["Capellini","dish/capellini"],["Carbonara","dish/carbonara"],["Carpaccio","dish/carpaccio"],["Cassoeula","dish/cassoeula"],["Ciabatta","dish/ciabatta"],["Cioppino","dish/cioppino"],["Coda alla vaccinara","dish/coda-alla-vaccinara"],["Colomba pasquale","dish/colomba-pasquale"],["Conti roll","dish/conti-roll"],["Cotoletta alla bolognese","dish/cotoletta-alla-bolognese"],["Crostata","dish/crostata"],["Drago restaurants","dish/drago-restaurants"],["Focaccia pugliese","dish/focaccia-pugliese"],["Frittata","dish/frittata"],["Giardiniera","dish/giardiniera"],["Guanciale","dish/guanciale"],["Italian restaurant","dish/italian-restaurant"],["Italian wedding soup","dish/italian-wedding-soup"],["Lampredotto","dish/lampredotto"],["Lasagna","dish/lasagna"],["List of Italian restaurants","dish/list-of-italian-restaurants"],["Marinara sauce","dish/marinara-sauce"],["Mondeghili","dish/mondeghili"],["Paccheri","dish/paccheri"],["Panini (sandwich)","dish/panini-sandwich"],["Panna cotta","dish/panna-cotta"],["Passatelli","dish/passatelli"],["Pecorino","dish/pecorino"]]}],"img":{"src":"/labs/cuisines/img/thumb/1/19/Minnuzze_di_sant%27aita.jpg/500px-Minnuzze_di_sant%27aita.jpg","w":500,"h":625,"by":"Stefano Mortellaro from Catania, Italy","lic":"CC BY 2.0","licUrl":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/2.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Minnuzze_di_sant'aita.jpg"}},"ingredient/lettuce":{"t":"ingredient","r":"ingredient/lettuce","n":"Lettuce","c":"#c9b8dd","crumb":[["Ingredients","ingredients"]],"sub":"Ingredient in 5 dishes across 3 cuisines","intro":[["Lettuce (Lactuca sativa) is an annual plant of the family Asteraceae mostly grown as a leaf vegetable. The leaves are most often used raw in green salads, although lettuce is also seen in other kinds of food, such as sandwiches, wraps and soups; it can also be grilled. Its stem and seeds are sometimes used; celtuce (asparagus lettuce) is one variety grown for its stems, which are eaten either raw or cooked. In addition to its main use as a leafy green, it has also gathered religious and medicinal significance over centuries of human consumption. Europe and North America originally dominated the market for lettuce, but by the late 20th century the consumption of lettuce had spread throughout the world. In 2023, world production of lettuce (and chicory) was 28 million tonnes, led by China with 53% of the total."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"ingredient-lettuce"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lettuce"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's cooked with","count":3,"groups":[["American cuisine","cuisine/american-cuisine",[["Club sandwich","dish/club-sandwich"],["Hamburger","dish/hamburger"],["Jibarito","dish/jibarito"]]],["Indonesian cuisine","cuisine/indonesian-cuisine",[["Selat solo","dish/selat-solo"]]],["Vietnamese cuisine","cuisine/vietnamese-cuisine",[["Shaking beef","dish/shaking-beef"]]]]},{"h":"Where it'